WEBVTT

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.560
When you get that late-night email that changes everything,

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.000
what is your first instinct reaction? Is it calm or chaos?

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.897
Resilience isn't about avoiding stress.

00:00:13.897 --> 00:00:18.240
It's about how you recover from it. It's a conditioned skill.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.720
In fact, recent studies from Stanford show that

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.240
real-time resilience can be seen in brain scans

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.950
revealing protective mechanisms that activate under extreme pressure.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.440
This tells us that

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.040
leaders who train their emotional recovery can regulate themselves more effectively

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.711
under chronic stress and make clearer decisions.

00:00:42.711 --> 00:00:46.000
For leaders, this means having a predefined

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.960
resilience playbook instead of go-to actions that help you stay grounded.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.920
This is a core component of your leadership impact on others because your team takes

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:05.040
their cues directly from you. A CEO I worked with had a simple but powerful play.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.615
When faced with a crisis,

00:01:07.615 --> 00:01:12.800
his first move is to never call a meeting. It's to take a 10-minute

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.754
walk. He says it allows him to separate the

00:01:15.754 --> 00:01:18.640
emotion from the data so he can return and

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.231
lead from a place of clarity, not reaction.

00:01:22.231 --> 00:01:26.240
Your play is to define your own resilience play.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:33.376
In your playbook, identify one stress trigger you frequently face.

00:01:33.376 --> 00:01:35.840
Then write down one simple

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.492
immediate action you can take to create a moment of pause before you respond.

00:01:41.492 --> 00:01:42.960
You're about to give

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.913
critical feedback to a sensitive team member

00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:48.720
and you feel your own stress levels rising.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.433
What is the one move from your resilience

00:01:52.433 --> 00:01:55.680
playbook you can use in the five minutes before

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.855
the conversation? Turn to the pre-conversation primer in your playbook.

00:02:00.855 --> 00:02:02.240
It provides a quick

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.756
drill to help you center yourself using resilience

00:02:05.756 --> 00:02:08.640
plays before any high-stakes interaction.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.240
Now that you've prepared yourself to handle pressure,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:18.480
let's talk about how you coach your team through it. I'll see you in the next video.
